  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nSlightly Less Breezy and Warmer End To The Weekend:\n\nWhile not as strong as Saturday, another day of breezy southwest \nwinds with gusts nearing 30+ mph primarily west of the Mississippi \nRiver Valley expected today. Upstream low level trougholysis near \nthe Missouri River Valley through Sunday causes a low level \nstretching axis to eventually succumb to anticyclonic flow through \nthe start of the week, resulting in slightly warmer temperatures, a \nfew degrees above normal by midweek, and lighter winds through the \nstart of the week.\n\nMostly Mild Week:\n\nHave lowered surface dewpoint temperatures a couple of degrees today \nbased on Saturday's observations and residual low level dry air. \nOtherwise, a band of slightly increased low level moisture will \nreside over the forecast area through the new week, tied between the \nexiting upper level low over Hudson Bay and return tropical flow \nthrough Southern to Central Plains. Main local impact will be bouts \nof increased cloud cover compared to currently forecasted NBM due to \npotential low bias given a maximum 40% Thursday outside of a \nsplotchy 40-50% for Monday. While currently forecasted precipitation \npotential remains less than 10% through midweek, recent LREF members \n(GEFS 17.18Z/EPS 17.18Z) have exhibited a 10-20% increase for \nmeasurable precipitation (0.01\") compared to their respective \nprevious runs, nearing 20-40%/10-30%, respectively Wednesday night \ninto Thursday morning. Minimal impact regardless given overall high \nconfidence in above freezing surface temperatures. \n\nWeekend Precipitation Potential:\n\nSubsequent more widespread precipitation chances reach the Upper \nMississippi River Valley this weekend as a quasi weak Omega block \nbreaks down and mid level positive vorticity advection lifts \nnortheast through the central CONUS. LREF member probabilities \nabstain certain probabilities well to the west/northwest of the \nforecast area closer to the area of strongest synoptic forcing with \n50% probabilities for 0.01\" of rainfall seen over the western half \nof the local forecast area.",
